{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>When he sins.</b> One must know for what matter the verse said ״כִּי תֶחֱטָא״, for it should have said only ״כִּי תִשְׁמַע קוֹל״ etc. And it appears that since this one was not required to be adjured with an oath in the first place — because he did not wish to testify to testimony that would benefit him — for at first his testimony is requested of him without an oath, and concerning his denial the verse calls it ״כִּי תֶחֱטָא״, for this is a sin — to speak lies. And if she heard a voice etc., she becomes liable to a sacrifice. And through this the vav of ״וְשָׁמְעָה״ is reconciled, for it indicates that besides this [i.e., the sin itself], it is the sin designated by the word חָטְאָה." "<b>Further,</b> it may be explained along the lines of what they said in the chapter *Shavu'at ha-Edut* (Shevuot 30a): they are liable for intentional oath and for its unwitting [violation] together with intentional testimony, but they are not liable for its unwitting [violation alone]. And Rashi explained: they are not liable for its unwitting [violation] — if they are completely unwitting, thinking they do not know testimony for him, etc. And the Rambam wrote in chapter 1 of *Hilkhot Shevu'ot*: What is unwitting violation of the oath of testimony? For example, it was hidden from him that one is liable to bring a sacrifice for it, but he knew that this oath is forbidden, etc., and that it is a lie — end quote. You see that the unwitting violation for which he is liable is one where he knows in any case that it is forbidden and a lie. And this is what the verse says: <b>a soul, when she sins</b> — meaning, whether intentionally or unwittingly, recognize that she is sinning — to exclude [the case where] she erred in thinking it is permitted, or forgot the testimony, for he is not liable for this oath. And in the *Aggadat Anshei Emet* (Zohar Vayikra 13a) this verse is expounded concerning the soul in its descent to the world, fittingly." "<b>And she hears a voice of oath.</b> It may be explained along the lines of what they said at the beginning of the chapter *Shavu'at ha-Edut* (Shevuot 30a), and this is the language of the Mishnah: the oath of testimony, etc. — whether before a court or not before a court, whether from his own mouth or from the mouth of others — they are not liable until they deny it in court; these are the words of Rabbi Meir. And the Sages say: whether from his own mouth or from the mouth of others — they are not liable until they deny it before a court — end quote. Now, according to Rabbi Meir, whenever it is in court, the oath need not be from his own mouth; rather, he becomes liable by the hearing of the ear alone. And this is what the verse says: <b>and she hears a voice of oath</b> — meaning, even though he did not answer amen." "And according to the Sages — following the reasoning of the Ra'avad and the later authorities — whoever heard a voice of oath, whether before a court or not before a court, even if he did not answer amen, as long as the denial was in court, even if not at the time of the oath, he is liable. And along this line the verse is precisely drawn, that in all cases it makes the matter contingent on hearing a voice of oath, and does not require an utterance of the lips." "<b>And the Rambam,</b> of blessed memory, although he ruled in accordance with the Sages that the denial must be in court, nevertheless distinguished: if the oath was not before a court, he must answer amen, as is clear from his words in chapter 9, halakha 1, and in chapter 10, halakha 17. If so, he holds that whenever it is not before a court, it is not effective from the mouth of others but only from his own mouth, or from the mouth of others and he answered amen — which is as though he uttered the oath from his own mouth, as he wrote at the beginning of chapter 2 of *Hilkhot Shevu'ot*. And the Ra'avad objected to him in chapter 9, halakha 10: the halakha does not follow Rabbi Meir, and moreover, whether according to Rabbi Meir or according to the Sages, what need is there for the court to be present at the time of the oath, for they are insistent, etc., only on the denial — end quote. And the Kesef Mishneh wrote that he did not know from where the Rambam derived this distinction." "<b>And to me</b> it appears that Rabbi Meir and the Rabbannan did not disagree except concerning one who swore of his own initiative outside of court, and the denial was not in court at all — for according to Rabbi Meir he is liable because he derives it from the oath of deposit, applying the principle of inference from it and from it: just as there, swearing of his own initiative renders him liable, so too from it even not in the presence of court he is liable; and the oath of testimony is likewise. But the Rabbannan hold: infer from it and leave it in its place, as explained there (Shevuot 31a). However, concerning one who swore at the initiative of others, not in the presence of court, and the denial was in court — there is no disagreement between them on this, and it implies that according to everyone he is liable." "<b>Rather,</b> the reason of the Rambam, who required for every oath that was not in the presence of court that it be of his own initiative, is from what they taught (Shevuot 31b), and this is their language: \"The oath of testimony — how so? He said to two: Come and testify for me. [They said:] We swear we do not know testimony for you, or they said: We do not know — I administer an oath upon you, and they said amen — these are liable. If he administered an oath upon them five times outside of court and they came to court and admitted — they are exempt; if they denied — they are liable for each one,\" etc., end quote. You see that it explicitly teaches \"and they said amen.\" Who is the author of this Mishnah? If Rabbi Meir — but then the denial was not in court, in which case it would constitute a dispute, and afterward an anonymous ruling, since in the Mishnah that preceded it, Rabbi [the redactor] wrote the disagreement of Rabbi Meir and the Rabbannan, and in this Mishnah that follows it he wrote anonymously according to Rabbi Meir. And from what we find that the Rambam required the denial in court even if one swore of his own initiative, it follows that he interprets the Mishnah according to the Rabbannan, and it teaches \"and they said amen\" — and in what case? Not in the presence of court, for if in the presence of court, according to all opinions there is no need for him to say amen. If so, you have it that according to the Sages he must say amen when not in the presence of court." "And the Tosafot wrote that the reason it says \"and they said amen\" is for brevity, and the same applies if they said \"we do not know testimony for you\"; but this is not the opinion of the Rambam, who holds that any oath that is outside of court requires him to say amen, meaning of his own initiative. And the reason is that Scripture did not say \"and he heard a voice\" even though he did not say it from his own mouth but in court, about which the verse speaks — as it is written \"if he does not declare\" — but when not in the presence of court, he must utter it from his mouth." "<b>And although</b> they said there (Shevuot 32b), and this is their language: \"From where do we know that denial in court is what renders liable, but outside does not render liable? Abaye said: The verse says 'if he does not declare' etc. — I said only in a place where if he declares, this one becomes liable. Rav Pappa said to him: If so, say that the oath itself must be in court — yes; not in court — no? That should not enter your mind, for it was taught in a baraita: 'for one' — to render liable for each and every one. And if it enters your mind that it must be in court, who renders liable for each and every one? And was it not taught: if he administered an oath upon them five times in the presence of court and they denied — they are not liable except for one, etc. Rather, must you not conclude from it that the oath is outside of court, [and] the denial is in court?\" — this implies that the oath that exists in court is itself the very oath that was outside of court, and one becomes liable on account of it; and if so, even if others administered the oath to him, there is no difference whether in the presence of court or not in the presence of court." "When we examine the truth of the matter from the flag of the column of the Rambam's law, z\"l — for from what he proves from the baraita of \"for one,\" to render liable for each and every one. And it is difficult: is not \"for one\" written in the context of the oath of utterance, while we are dealing with the oath of testimony, and this oath is not comparable to that one. And it is compelling to say that he proves only concerning an oath of one's own initiative, which was derived as existing within the oath of testimony from the oath of utterance by means of a gezeirah shavah of \"תחטא תחטא\" as explained there, and therefore he proves well for the oath of testimony in the case of one who swears of his own mouth, which is derived from it; but if others administered the oath to him — which is the oath mentioned in the passage of the oath of testimony — it is specifically in court that one is liable on its account, for he proves nothing at all from \"for one,\" and we return to what Rav Pappa inferred: \"in court — yes; not in court — no.\" And Abaye did not reject the words of Rav Pappa except regarding what he inferred from his words, \"the oath etc. not in court — no,\" which implies that liability for the oath of testimony is only in the presence of court; this Abaye rejected from his mind, and in any case when sworn by others it is specifically in court, according to the opinion of the Rambam." "<b>We thus find ourselves saying</b> according to this approach, that the verse's saying <b>and she heard the voice of an oath</b> refers to where they administered an oath to him, and even though he did not answer Amen, and specifically in a court, as the verse is precise: ״if he does not tell it.״ And if he uttered an oath from his own mouth, he is likewise liable for the particular oath that took effect upon him in this matter, by way of a gezerah shavah of ״תֶּחֱטָא תֶּחֱטָא״ from the oath of expression (shavuat bitui), on the condition that the denial be in court. And what the Gemara concludes (Shevuot 32b): ״infer from this that an oath outside of court [is binding]״ — meaning, there is such a thing as an oath outside of court, and it is in the case of an oath from his own mouth." "<b>And now</b> we have successfully resolved the plain sense of the verse according to the Rambam's view, correctly — that the verse speaks of a single scenario, namely, that they administer an oath to him in court and he does not tell it in court, and these two things are both required: the oath administered by others must be in court, and outside of court it does not apply, even if the denial was in court at a later time; and likewise the denial must also be in court. And without this, even if he swore of his own accord — which they derived from ״תֶּחֱטָא תֶּחֱטָא״ from the oath of expression — he is not liable. And in a case where the denial was in court but the oath was not in the presence of a court, we derive from the oath of expression that if he swore with his own mouth he is liable. And the discerning one will understand that the Rambam's ruling shines clear, and the verse supports him. And one who claims that an oath administered by others is effective outside of court must bring proof, for the hermeneutic derivation from ״לְאַחַת״ applies only to one who swears of his own accord, or others administered an oath to him and he said Amen — which amounts to of his own mouth."], :book :leviticus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 5, :notes {6 "Resolved רַבִּי in the phrase מַנִּי מַתְנִיתִין as referring to Rabbi Yehudah HaNasi, the redactor of the Mishnah, per the standard Talmudic usage in this sugya.", 7 "The word \"הגם\" at the opening appears to belong syntactically to the following comment [3] rather than this one; translated here as it stands in the text.", 8 "The opening word וַהֲגַם (\"and although\") signals that this comment raises an objection to the preceding interpretation.", 10 "The citation ד״תֶחֱטָא תֶּחֱטָא״ appears to open with a possibly misplaced or corrupted ד״ mark (possibly דִּ, 'that of'); translated hyper-literally as part of the gezerah shavah formula."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:54:04.248071333Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}